## [0.6.2] — Numbers bar: one object, not a bolted-on band

0.6.1 fixed the muddy panels by giving the rail its own opaque navy band. That
solved the colour and created a worse problem: the band read as a **second
section bolted to the bottom of the hero**, with three boxes floating in a
slab of dead navy. The hero stopped being one object.

Corrected by going the other way. The separate band is gone and the three
panels are now **a single glass bar** sitting directly on the photograph:

- One `<dl>` carrying `.glass-dark`, split into three cells by hairline
  dividers rather than three separate boxes with gaps between them.
- A single accent rule spanning the whole bar instead of one per panel.
- Cells stack vertically below `sm`, with the dividers flipping from vertical
  to horizontal.
- The bottom navy fade added in 0.6.1 was removed, so the photograph now runs
  to the bottom of the hero with the bar sitting on it.

The muddiness that prompted the band in the first place is handled by the
panel surface itself, which 0.6.1 already took to `rgba(0,31,57,0.72)` — dense
enough to stay cool and even over any part of the photograph. The band was
never the thing doing that work.

### Verified
Single container confirmed (one `<dl>`, one `.glass-dark`); separate navy band
gone; 3 cells at 173px each; accent rule 1183px across an 1184px bar;
`backdrop-filter: blur(24px) saturate(1.5)` active; 64px of photograph visible
below the bar. 320px: bar 288px wide, cells stacked, both figures fit, no page
overflow, 0 broken images. `tsc --noEmit` clean, `next build` static.

