## [0.6.0] — Premium surface layer, emphasis, navbar retailer mark

### Navbar
"Authorized Retailer" now sits under the wordmark as part of the logo lockup.
Hidden below `sm` so it cannot crowd a 320px header — the teal disclosure bar
directly above already states the same thing at every width.

### Emphasis: the numbers now carry weight
The hero rail was three cells split by hairlines, which gave the rate, the top
speed and the included install no more presence than body copy. Rebuilt as
three **glass panels** — gradient surface, real border, highlight inset,
backdrop blur, a panning accent rail along the top edge, and a lift on hover.

The numerals inside them are gradient-filled with a bloom: `stat-gradient`
(teal → sky) on the speed and install figures, `stat-gradient-sprout` on the
rate, which `PriceLockup`'s dark tone now uses. Both use `drop-shadow` rather
than `text-shadow`, for the same reason the headline sheen does — the fill is
a gradient clipped to the glyphs, so the text itself is transparent.

### Headline
Wrapped in `.headline-bloom`, which paints a soft radial light behind the type
through a `::before` at `z-index: -1`, so the headline sits in its own pool of
light rather than flat on the photograph. A panning `accent-rule` closes the
block, and the same rule now sits under every section heading.

### Sections
- Section titles take `heading-gradient` (navy-dark → navy → navy-700, all
  dark enough that contrast on ivory is never in question).
- Eyebrows became pills with a pulsing dot.
- Plan cards: gradient surfaces, a panning accent rail along the top edge
  (always on for the featured card, on hover for the rest), teal-bordered
  speed readouts, badges moved to the top-right with a coloured glow, and a
  `glow-hover` ring.

### The hero scrim had to be re-architected
Hardening contrast in 0.5.0 pushed the scrim to `via/72 to/28`, and that
buried the photograph completely — exactly what had been asked to avoid.

The root cause was structural, not a matter of tuning: **the scrim was
anchored to the viewport while the copy is centred in a max-width shell**, so
the distance from the screen edge to the last word changes with window size.
Stops tuned for one width either fail contrast at another or go so dark the
photo disappears. There was no value that worked everywhere.

Fixed with `.copy-scrim`, a `::before` pinned to the copy column itself,
extending `-50vw` to the left and `-7rem` past its right edge. The falloff now
lands in the same place relative to the text at every width. The full-width
layers could then drop right back — radial to `md:opacity-[0.14]`, flat tint
to `/10`.

The copy column also narrowed from `max-w-4xl` to `max-w-3xl`, which both
reads more editorial and pulls the falloff left so more of the frame opens up.

Result at 1265px: photograph clearly visible from ~63% of the frame, and

| | Needs | Measured |
|---|---|---|
| H1 | 3.0 | 4.35 |
| Subline | 4.5 | 6.41 |
| ZIP helper | 4.5 | 7.43 |

### Bug: the build silently strips `backdrop-filter`
`.glass-dark` declared `backdrop-filter` and it never reached the browser —
the emitted rule came back carrying only `background`, `border` and
`box-shadow`, and `getComputedStyle` reported `none` despite the browser
supporting it. The glass panels were rendering flat.

Fixed by applying Tailwind's own `backdrop-blur-md backdrop-saturate-150`
utilities on the element, which survive the build. Confirmed afterwards:
`blur(12px) saturate(1.5)`. A note sits in `globals.css` so it does not get
re-added to the custom rule and silently fail again.

### Note on verifying in the app's browser pane
The pane scales an emulated viewport down to fit, which lowers the effective
DPR, so `next/image` picks a much smaller variant — a 528x288 file was being
stretched into a 1425x1029 box, which reads as a blurred gradient rather than
a photograph. Real displays request the full 1408 source. Worth knowing before
concluding an image "is not showing".

### Verified
3 glass panels, 3 gradient numerals, 19 accent rules, 6 gradient headings,
8 glow-hover cards, retailer mark present. 320px: no overflow, zero offenders,
0 broken images. `tsc --noEmit` clean, `next build` static.

---

## [0.5.0] — Ambient motion system

Requested: a more animated, premium hero, and animated colour gradients behind
the other sections. This reverses the 0.1.0 "keep animations minimal" line, so
the later instruction was taken as current. The hard negatives still hold —
everything below is CSS keyframes, no WebGL, no 3D, no animated diagrams.
`bestfiberinternet.us` was read first; it runs the same approach (`spcAurora`
16s, `spcBeam` 12s, `spcFloat` 7-9.5s staggered, one-shot `spcRise` with
d1/d2/d3), all CSS.

### What was added
| Animation | Where | Detail |
|---|---|---|
| `aurora-a/b/c` | 8 sections | Three brand blobs, 19s/23s/27s — no shared period, so the loop never reads as a loop |
| `beam-sweep` | Hero | Light band crossing, 14s |
| `hero-rise` | Hero | One-shot entrance, 0.9s, staggered 0/110/220/330/430ms |
| `text-sheen` | Hero accent | Sprout-to-teal sweep via `background-clip: text`, 9s |
| `chip-float` | Trust marks | 7s, offset 900ms apart |
| `dot-pulse` | Eyebrow dot | 2.4s |

New `components/Aurora.tsx` renders the colour field with `dark`/`light` tones
and an optional dot lattice. Everything animates `transform` or `opacity` only,
so it composites without layout or paint, and the whole set stops under
`prefers-reduced-motion`.

### Contrast had to be re-derived, and it failed first
The moving fields lift the hero canvas, which invalidated the scrim measured
in 0.4.0. Re-measured with the aurora modelled at its maximum uniform alpha.

A first pass measured each text block's **bounding box**, which was wrong —
short final lines ("CAPS.") leave empty space that gets sampled as if it held
type, so everything looked like a failure. Re-measured against real glyph
rectangles via `Range.getClientRects()`:

| | Needs | Box (wrong) | Glyph rects | After fix |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| H1 | 3.0 | 2.71 | 3.49 ✓ | 4.94 |
| Subline | 4.5 | 4.09 | **3.84 ✗** | 5.08 |
| ZIP helper | 4.5 | 3.29 | **4.41 ✗** | 7.11 |

Two real failures. Fixed by:
- Hero scrim `via-navy-dark/60 to-transparent` → `via-navy-dark/72
  to-navy-dark/28`. The copy column reaches ~70% of the viewport at mid widths
  (~1000px), where the old gradient had already thinned to nothing. A floor on
  the right holds contrast and still leaves the photograph plainly visible.
- ZIP helper `text-ivory/65` → `/85`.
- `.text-on-media` text-shadow on hero copy, so legibility does not depend on
  where the drifting field happens to be.

Light sections were checked against the impossible worst case — all three
blobs stacked at full strength — and still pass: body copy 4.95, headings 7.30.

### Two bugs found while verifying
**1. The sheen rendered muddy grey.** `.text-on-media` sets `text-shadow`, but
the sheen fills glyphs with a gradient and sets `color: transparent`. The
shadow paints a dark blurred copy that bleeds through the antialiased edges.
Swapped to `filter: drop-shadow()`, which works off rendered alpha.

**2. `.hero-rise` could hide the headline permanently.** It set `opacity: 0`
in the rule and animated to 1. If the animation never runs — unsupported,
blocked, stripped — the hero headline is invisible for good. This is the same
failure `.reveal` was hardened against in 0.1.0 and I reintroduced it.

Fixed by moving the hidden state out of the rule and into the keyframe's
`from`, with `animation-fill-mode: both` applying it backwards through the
delay. With no animation the element keeps its default opacity of 1. Verified
by setting `animation: none` on the live H1 and confirming opacity resolves
to 1.

(Separately: measurements taken while the app window is hidden report
`opacity: 0` because the browser freezes `document.timeline` — `currentTime`
and `documentTimelineTime` both sit at 0. That is a test artifact, not a site
bug; the animation runs when the tab is visible.)

### Perf
Blob blur cut from `blur-3xl` (64px) to `blur-xl` (24px). Eight fields means
24 blurred layers to rasterise, and the radial gradients already fall off to
transparent on their own — most of that radius was doing no visual work. A
small radius is kept to avoid banding on the flat ivory sections.

### Verified
70 animated elements resolve across the page. 320px: no overflow, zero
offenders, 0 broken images. `tsc --noEmit` clean, `next build` static.

