## [0.4.0] — Redundancy audit, lighter hero scrim, phone backdrop slot

### Audit: the page made the same argument three times
Requested check of whether the three photo bands were earning their place
against the existing Why NextLight and How It Works sections. They were not:

| Feature band | Already said by |
|---|---|
| Installation | whyUs "Free professional installation" **and** howItWorks "Get installed" |
| At Home | whyUs "No caps, no throttling" + whyUs "Room to grow" |
| Working From Home | whyUs "Uploads match downloads" |

Some bullets were near-verbatim duplicates — "Move up a tier at any time
without changing networks" in the band against "move up a tier without
changing networks" in the whyUs body.

**Resolution — merge rather than delete.** The bands carry the photography and
say more per point, so they were kept and the six-card grid in `#why` was
removed. `FeatureBands` no longer renders its own `<section>`; it renders
inside `#why` under the whyUs heading. `whyUs.points` is gone from
`lib/content.ts`, leaving only the eyebrow, title and description that head
the section. The `#why` anchor and its nav link are unaffected.

Band 1 was still duplicating How It Works, so it was repurposed from
installation to **the network itself** — community ownership, fiber end to
end, local crews. It keeps the technician photograph, which suits that story.
How It Works now owns the installation narrative alone.

Net effect: each argument appears once. The page lost a section and roughly
half a screen of restated copy.

### Hero scrim lightened, and a contrast regression caught
The photograph was barely readable under the 0.3.1 scrim. Loosened to
`md:opacity-25` on the radial and `from-navy-dark via-navy-dark/60
to-transparent` on the linear (was `/80` to `/25`), plus the md–lg floor
dropped from `/40` to `/25`.

That is a real accessibility risk, so contrast was measured rather than eyed:
the hero photo was drawn to a canvas, sampled along each text block, and the
scrim layers composited in software to get the true background behind the
type — then each element's own text opacity applied on top.

Result at 1425px: H1 fine, but the **subline measured 4.21:1, under the 4.5:1
AA needs for body text**, at the right-hand end where the gradient thins.
Fixed by raising it from `text-ivory/75` to `text-ivory/90`.

| Element | Needs | Before | After |
|---|---|---|---|
| H1 (large text) | 3.0 | 4.18 | 3.72 ✓ |
| Subline (body) | 4.5 | **4.21 ✗** | 6.83 ✓ |
| ZIP helper (body) | 4.5 | 5.38 | 5.38 ✓ |

### Digital Voice backdrop slot
The phone section holds one card on a wide band and read thin beside the
multi-card sections. `ServiceSections` now renders a full-bleed photographic
backdrop for the `phone` line when `phoneBackground` resolves, under an 80%
navy scrim — heavier than the hero's because content spans the full width
here rather than hugging the left.

`SectionHeading` gained a `tone` prop for this: `dark` switches the eyebrow to
teal, the rule to sprout and the title to ivory. The section falls back to the
plain ivory band while the file is absent, so nothing is broken in the
meantime.

Prompt and filename (`home-phone-background.jpg`, 1408x768) added to
`public/images/README.md`.

### Verified
- `#feature-bands` no longer exists as a section; 3 bands render inside
  `#why`; the old card grid returns 0 items.
- Section order unchanged: hero, fiber, bundles, phone, fine-print,
  how-it-works, why, faq.
- 4 images on the page, 0 broken. Phone section still plain ivory pending its
  photo.
- `tsc --noEmit` clean, `next build` static.

