## [0.1.0] — Initial build

### Stack
- Next.js 16.3.5 (App Router, Turbopack), React 19.2, TypeScript 5.
- Tailwind CSS v4 (CSS-first `@theme`, no `tailwind.config.js`).
- `lenis` for smooth scrolling. No other runtime dependencies.
- Strict frontend build: no API routes, no database, no server actions.
  The ZIP checker resolves entirely in the browser against a static list.

### Architecture decisions

**1. `lib/content.ts` is the single source of truth.**
Every price, speed, fee, feature bullet, disclosure string, nav label, FAQ
entry and phone number lives in this one file. No TSX file contains a literal
price, speed or phone number. Changing a rate there cascades automatically to:
- the hero price anchor (`leadPlan()` picks the lowest-priced fiber tier),
- every plan card,
- the fine-print comparison table (rows generated from the same `plans` array),
- the hero speed stat (`topSpeedPlan()`),
- the CTA label on each card (`ctaLabel()` returns "Call to order" when a price
  exists, "Call for pricing" when it does not).

**1a. A single rate card feeds every dollar figure.**
Refined after the first cascade test found a weakness: bundle qualifier
sentences originally repeated `$39.95` / `$12.95` as literal prose, so editing
a plan price left the explanation stale. All dollar amounts now originate in
one `rates` object and are formatted through `splitRate()`, `money()` and
`monthly()`. Everything downstream is derived:
- fiber plan prices spread from `splitRate(rates.*)`,
- bundle totals are computed sums (`fiberComplete1Gig + wifiSingleFamily`),
- bundle qualifier sentences are template literals over the same rates, so the
  price and the sentence explaining it can never disagree,
- `finePrint.addOns` costs and the two FAQ answers that quote a fee read the
  rate card as well.

Verified end to end: changing `rates.wifiSingleFamily` from 12.95 to 19.99
updated both WiFi bundle card prices ($52.90 → $59.94, $82.90 → $89.94), both
bundle qualifier sentences, both fine-print table rows, the add-on cost tile
and the router FAQ answer — with no TSX file touched. Reverted after the test.

**2. Service line order is data-driven, not hard-coded.**
`serviceSectionOrder` fixes the canonical merchandising sequence
`fiber → cable → bundle → tv → mobile → phone`. `activeServiceSections()`
filters that list to lines that actually have plans. `ServiceSections.tsx`
maps the result, so a line with zero plans is omitted completely — no empty
section, no placeholder card. Adding plans for a new line to `lib/content.ts`
makes its section appear in the correct position with no layout edits.

**3. `<PriceLockup plan={plan} />` is the only price renderer.**
One component, consumed by the hero and every card. Renders a flex row:
dollar sign, dominant integer (2.5rem `card` / 3.5rem `hero`), muted cents,
`/mo` term. Reads `price`, `cents` and `promoQualifier` off the PlanItem.
Falls back to the "Call for pricing" label when `price` is undefined. Has a
`dark` tone variant for use on navy surfaces. `aria-label` carries the full
spoken price since the numerals are split across spans.

**4. `<CallButton />` is the only outbound call link.**
Guarantees the number always comes from `site.phoneTel` and that every anchor
carries `data-call-cta` for call tracking. The footer's large number link is
the one hand-written `tel:` anchor and also carries the attribute.

### Brand system
Palette taken verbatim from the CSS custom properties published on
mynextlight.com — not approximated:

| Token | Hex | Use |
|---|---|---|
| `--color-navy-dark` | `#002C51` | hero background, footer, disclosure bar |
| `--color-navy` | `#004A81` | primary buttons, headings, price integers |
| `--color-teal` | `#3ECEBA` | accents, ZIP submit, check marks |
| `--color-sky` | `#4CCAEF` | hover states, focus ring |
| `--color-sprout` | `#D6F38E` | "New" plan badge |
| `--color-ivory` | `#FFFFF6` | type on dark surfaces |
| `--color-slate-ink` | `#3E4B56` | body copy |
| `--color-mist` | `#D8DBDD` | borders, dividers |

`--color-navy-050/100/700` are derived tints for surfaces and alternating
section backgrounds. Typography matches the official site: Poppins (display)
and Noto Sans (body), loaded via `next/font/google`.

### Animation budget
Deliberately minimal — no WebGL, no 3D backgrounds, no animated diagrams.
- Lenis smooth scroll (`duration: 1.05`), with anchor clicks intercepted so
  in-page navigation uses the same easing and offsets by `--header-offset`.
- `<Reveal />`: one-shot entrance fade + 18px rise. The IntersectionObserver
  unobserves on first intersection, so nothing replays on scroll-back.
- Card hover: 1px lift plus shadow/border transition. Nothing else.
- Everything is disabled under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`; Lenis is not
  instantiated at all in that case.

**Reveal failure modes, and how they are handled.** An entrance reveal that
starts at `opacity: 0` will hide the page outright if its JavaScript never
runs. Two guards, both pure CSS — an earlier inline-script approach was
dropped because mutating `<html>`'s class list before hydration produced a
React hydration mismatch:
1. The hidden start state lives inside `@media (scripting: enabled)`, so a
   no-JS visitor is served the finished layout.
2. A `reveal-failsafe` keyframe un-hides every element after 3s, covering the
   case where scripting is on but the bundle fails to load. `.is-visible`
   sets `animation: none`, so the normal path is untouched.

### Content sourcing
Plans, rates and terms audited against the official NextLight site:
- Fiber: Essential 100 Mbps $39.95 · Complete 1 Gig $69.95 (popular) ·
  Advanced 2.5 Gig $149.95 (new) · Ultimate 8 Gig $249.95 (new). All
  symmetrical, no caps, no contract, free professional installation.
- Whole-Home WiFi: $12.95/mo single family (U6), $8.95/mo apartment (U4),
  mesh units $4.95/mo each.
- Digital Voice: from $25.00/mo, unlimited nationwide calling,
  voicemail-to-email, simultaneous ring, Find Me / Follow Me, $5 port fee.
- Bundle rows combine the published monthly rate of each component service;
  each bundle's `promoQualifier` states the component rates explicitly.

**Omitted service lines:** `cable`, `tv` and `mobile` carry zero plans because
NextLight does not offer them natively. Their sections therefore never render.

### Page order (as built)
1. Persistent disclosure bar — "Independent Authorized Retailer of NextLight."
2. Sticky header — wordmark, anchor nav (Plans · Why NextLight · FAQ),
   solid call button.
3. Hero — eyebrow, H1, subline, trust chips, speed stats; order card with the
   price anchor, ZIP checker **and** a "Call to order" button.
4. Fiber plans.
5. Bundles.
6. Digital Voice home phone.
7. Fine-print grid — full comparison table + hardware/one-time costs.
8. How It Works, then Why NextLight, with a mid-page conversion band.
9. FAQ — native `<details>` accordion, works before hydration.
10. Footer — four columns, legal rail, retailer disclosure.

### Compliance constraints enforced
- No copy routing users to the carrier for billing, outages or support.
- Hero keeps both the ZIP checker and the call button.
- Card and section CTAs read "Call to order" / "Call for pricing" only. Raw
  phone numbers appear solely in the header and footer.
- No competitor comparisons and no self-deprecating retailer copy.
- No sections for services NextLight does not offer; no placeholder cards.
- No Broadband Facts label links anywhere.
- No "pricing observed [date]" strings anywhere.
- No "is this the official site?" FAQ and no reseller-structure Q&A.
- Retailer disclosure appears in the top bar, the fine-print section and the
  footer legal rail.

### Layout safety
- `html, body { max-width: 100%; overflow-x: hidden }` plus a global
  `* { min-width: 0 }` so flex and grid children can shrink.
- The fine-print table is the only wide element; it scrolls inside its own
  `.scroll-x` container rather than pushing the page.

### Verification results
Measured against the running app, not assumed:
- **320px overflow:** `scrollWidth === clientWidth === 320` at three scroll
  depths, with zero elements extending past the viewport (the fine-print
  table's own `.scroll-x` scroller excluded by design).
- **Call tracking:** 12 `tel:` anchors on the page, all 12 carrying
  `data-call-cta`, all resolving to the single `site.phoneTel` value.
- **CTA labels:** the only label on a call link outside the header/footer is
  "Call to order". No raw phone number appears in the page body.
- **Section order:** `hero, fiber, bundles, phone, fine-print, how-it-works,
  why, faq` — cable, tv and mobile absent as intended.
- **Banned-phrase sweep:** date stamps, Broadband Facts links, "is this the
  official site" FAQ, "ordering channel", "terrestrial fiber", "call to ask"
  — all absent.
- **Console:** clean on a fresh load, no errors or warnings.
- **Build:** `next build` succeeds; the route is fully static (`○`).

Note on a test artifact: measuring reveal state by driving `window.scrollTo`
gives a false reading, because Lenis restores its own scroll target on the
next frame and the page never actually moves. Verify with real wheel input.
